WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:02.640
Government regulation of land

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.790
in the western United States on balance,

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:07.210
doesn't protect the
environment from industry.

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:09.650
It protects industry
from having to compete

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:10.840
in the free market.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.680
Why can't environmentalists
acquire the land

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:14.710
that they want to preserve?

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:16.640
Often, because it's illegal.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.750
Shawn Reagan is the vice
president of research

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:21.330
at the Property and
Environment Research Center,

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:22.920
which is a leading advocacy group

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:25.320
in the free-market
environmentalist movement.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.010
He says preservation groups
are often willing to compete

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:30.160
with industry on a level playing field.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:31.930
They are looking to use markets,

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:34.680
to use property rights,
to use voluntary exchange,

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.330
to essentially get what they want,

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.700
which is preserving natural amenities,

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.650
conserving wildlife and open space.

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:42.810
So in the case of public lands,

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:46.480
we see groups trying to acquire
rancher's grazing permits,

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:50.360
we see groups trying to
buy federal energy leases

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:52.170
to keep the resources in the ground.

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:56.030
We even see groups trying
to bid for timber leases,

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:59.920
but to keep the trees standing
rather than harvesting them.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:01.640
In many cases, though,
they've been thwarted

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:05.593
by rules that basically make that illegal.

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.130
Most people think of
public lands in the west

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:11.940
as being national parks
or wilderness areas,

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.770
but really most of the lands
in the west are managed

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:17.320
by other federal agencies

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:19.400
and they're fundamentally political lands.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.350
Reagan says that these
lands are often leased

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:22.430
through public auctions,

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:24.520
but environmental groups that
want to pay for the right

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.980
to preserve them are frequently
excluded for participating.

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:30.740
Too often, natural resource
management is biased

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:34.880
towards resource extraction
because rules that were created

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:39.000
in the 19th century essentially
require rightsholders

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.350
to harvest, extract, or graze the resource

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:45.240
rather than to conserve it.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:46.380
Because federal agencies

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:49.540
don't consider conservation
to be a beneficial public use,

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.520
environmental groups are locked out.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.240
Reagan says that in a free market,

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:54.920
the highest bidders should win

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.580
even if their intention is
to leave the land untouched.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:00.800
Our idea of a beneficial
use is much broader.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.230
A lot of times the highest valued use

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:04.840
would be a community
coming together to decide

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:09.560
that a forest should be left
standing rather than harvested,

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:12.090
or conserved for some
recreational opportunity.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:13.280
Reagan points to one case

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:15.750
in which environmentalist
successfully preserved land

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.860
in Bozeman, Montana by
outbidding the competition.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:21.410
The state offered up a timber sale

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:24.340
on some state-owned lands, south of town,

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:27.930
and a community group formed
to oppose this project.

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:31.320
But rather than trying
to litigate or regulate,

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:33.620
they attempted to outbid
the timber company

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.860
instead of harvesting the trees.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:38.210
And the local group raised enough money

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:40.650
to purchase this lease.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:42.660
They outbid the timber company.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:44.520
And for 25 years,

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.970
they have the right to
keep these trees standing.

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:49.540
And yet the Montana
Legislature's response was

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:52.050
to change the law to prevent
environmentalist groups

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.960
from competing against timber
companies in the future.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:58.210
In the case of the Bozeman timber sale,

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:02.540
the community group paid more
than the logging company.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:05.780
So taxpayers in Montana earn
more from this nonuse right

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:08.770
than they would have from a timber sale.

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:11.823
And I think that's an important
distinction here is that,

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:15.420
just because we're leaving trees standing

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.510
doesn't mean that we're
not going to generate

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:18.680
as much revenue.

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:20.550
It's, in fact, the only
way that this lease

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:21.510
would have moved forward,

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:24.770
is if the group outbid
the logging company.

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:28.110
The lease that the group
purchased is good for 25 years.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:30.120
And so after the 25-year period,

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.380
they'd have to acquire
another similar lease

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:35.210
and the state would stand
to earn even more revenue

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:36.900
from another Conservation License.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.810
Whereas, if the lease had
gone to the logging company,

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:40.830
it would take a hundred years

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:42.310
before that forest would regrow

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:45.160
to the point where it could
potentially be harvested again.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:47.780
By excluding environmental
groups, Reagan says,

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:50.060
we get a distorted picture about the value

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:51.720
of our natural resources.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.600
The point is not that
resource development is bad.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:58.040
It's not that extracting
energy is fundamentally wrong

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:01.810
or harvesting timber
is fundamentally wrong.

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:04.970
We all live in a house
likely that's made of wood.

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:08.930
But rather, it's that
environmental values are real

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:09.910
and they're legitimate

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:13.210
and we need to find ways
to allow for those values

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:15.380
to be expressed through markets

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:17.310
and through voluntary exchange.

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:18.940
I think a system of non-use rights

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:22.250
where markets can help resolve
these competing demands

00:04:23.487 --> 00:04:27.070
is a system that can help us
allocate resource extraction

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:29.690
to places where it makes the most sense

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:32.803
and do conservation where it
makes the most sense as well.

